\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{A cofactor polynomial for the dressed pair}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Use the rank-one sign links on the #{2\times2} torus of
\ref{fdip-00U9}, over any commutative ring #{k} with the #{5}
specified involutions. Let #{F_z=k} at every site #{z} and
take #{K=5\bbI+\extD_U} on #{\mathcal V=C^\bullet(X;k)}. Set #{x=(0,0)}, #{y=(1,0)} and let
#{\gamma:y\to x} reverse the positive tree edge from #{x} to #{y}.
For the degree-zero inclusion #{\iota_x:F_x\to\mathcal V},
projection #{\pr_y:\mathcal V\to F_y} and transport
#{h_\gamma(U):F_y\to F_x}, the unnormalized coefficient of
the barred-first \vocabk{dressed pair}{fdip-00WD} with identity
insertion is
##{\begin{aligned}
&-\trace_{F_x}\bigl(h_\gamma(U)\pr_y(\adjugate K)\iota_x\bigr)\\
&\qquad=Q(a,b,c,d,e),
\end{aligned}}
where
##{\begin{aligned}
 Q(a,b,c,d,e)={}&412470750(1+a)\\
 &-4708635(bd+abd)+2803395(b+d)\\
 &-1909890(c+ac+e+ae)\\
 &+1866110(ab+ad+bce+cde)\\
 &+928825(abce+acde)\\
 &+915555(bc+be+cd+de)\\
 &+30390(ce+ace)\\
 &-21730(abc+abe+acd+ade)\\
 &+17280(bcd+abcd+bde+abde)\\
 &+12750(bcde+abcde).
\end{aligned}}}

\proof{
\p{Work in the integral sign ring #{R} of \ref{fdip-00U9}.
Both parity modules have rank #{8}. In their cellular bases,
let #{t,s} be indeterminates, and perturb the even block
of the mass-#{t} kernel by #{sE_{ij}} for a matrix unit
#{E_{ij}} on even cochains. Eliminating the odd block #{t\bbI} after inverting #{t}
gives
##{\begin{aligned}
&\det\begin{pmatrix}t\bbI+sE_{ij}&C^{\mathsf T}\\C&t\bbI\end{pmatrix}\\
&\qquad=\det(t^2\bbI -C^{\mathsf T}C+stE_{ij}).
\end{aligned}}
Indeed the odd block contributes #{t^8}, which clears the
denominators of the even block #{t\bbI+sE_{ij}-t^{-1}C^{\mathsf T}C}.
Both determinants are polynomials in #{R[t,s]}; multiplication
by the indeterminate #{t} is injective, so the identity already
holds there. The linear coefficient of #{s} is the cofactor
selected by #{E_{ij}} on the left and #{t} times the corresponding
cofactor on the right. Varying #{i,j} and setting #{t=5} proves
that the even-even block of #{\adjugate K} equals
#{5\adjugate M} over #{R}, with #{M=25\bbI -C^\dagger C}.}

\p{The chosen link is one, so its reverse transport is one.
The fiber-trace formula of \ref{fdip-00WG} selects the negative
of the adjugate entry with output at #{y} and input at #{x}.
In the order of \ref{fdip-00U9} these are indices #{1,0}
counting from zero. The entry #{(\adjugate M)_{1,0}} deletes
row zero and column one of #{M}; write #{N} for the remaining
matrix, keeping its order. The cofactor sign is
#{(-1)^{0+1}=-1}. The barred-first minus sign therefore
gives #{5\det N}.}

\p{Relabel the rows and columns of #{N} by
#{1,\ldots,7}. For
#{S=\{j_1<\cdots<j_r\}\subseteq\{1,\ldots,7\}},
set
##{E(\varnothing)=1,\qquad
 E(S)=\sum_{\ell=1}^r(-1)^{r+\ell}
 N_{r,j_\ell}E(S\setminus\{j_\ell\}).}
Expansion in the last row identifies #{E(S)} with
the minor of the first #{r} rows and columns #{S};
induction starts at the empty minor. Hence
#{5E(\{1,\ldots,7\})=5\det N}.}

\p{As in \ref{fdip-00U9}, the squarefree words are
an integer basis of #{R}, and multiplying two words
takes the symmetric difference of their letter sets.
Substituting the displayed #{H,J} of that lemma into
#{N} and applying this recurrence yields the
displayed coefficients of #{Q}. This finite
cofactor expansion proves the identity over #{R};
any assignment of #{5} commuting involutions
preserves it.}
}
